-- The inertia theorem at a concrete ring: the integers as canonical binary numbers, a datatype of
-- normal forms (zero, a positive, a negative; a positive is 1, 2p or 2p+1), so that equal integers are
-- equal terms and the ring laws are paths. Addition and multiplication run through the binary integers
-- of chem/bareisswit.cub and back, so their cost is binary arithmetic's. The laws are proved by
-- transfer: the value of a member as a difference of two naturals (zr-val), the operations'
-- agreement with the differences' (zr-had, zr-hmu), the identities on differences by the semiring
-- procedure of lib/ring.cub, and the value's injectivity (zr-inj). With the twelve laws (zr-DL) and
-- the two sign bundles (zr-QN, zr-QP), it-count-matrix applies: the count of negative directions a
-- checked witness shows is the matrix's (inertia-cert-sound).
import "bareisswit.cub"
import "../lib/linproof.cub"
import "../lib/ring.cub"

